.TH HOWDY 1 "@HOWDY_MAN_DATE@" "Howdy Next @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
howdy \- facial authentication management for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B howdy
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIarguments\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Howdy manages enrolled face models and camera-based recognition for Howdy Next.
Recognition is exposed to authentication services through the
.BR pam_howdy (8)
module.
.PP
The command selects a management operation. A target user is resolved from the
invoking environment when possible; use the user option when explicit targeting
is required.
.SH COMMANDS
@HOWDY_COMMAND_REFERENCE@
.SH OPTIONS
@HOWDY_OPTION_REFERENCE@
.SH EXAMPLES
Add a face model, test recognition, or edit configuration:
.PP
.nf
sudo howdy add
sudo howdy test
sudo howdy config
.fi
.PP
Use
.BR \-U
or
.BR \-\-user
when a model command must target a specific account.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @HOWDY_CONFIG_PATH_ROFF@
Main Howdy configuration file.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
Packaged ONNX model directory.
.TP
.I @HOWDY_USER_MODELS_DIR_ROFF@
Configured directory containing enrolled user face models.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The command returns zero on success and a nonzero status when parsing, validation,
recognition, or storage fails.
.SH "SECURITY NOTES"
Facial recognition is weaker than a password. Similar faces or photographs may
fool it.
.B Do not use Howdy as the sole authentication method.
Keep the configured files and model directories root-controlled.
.SH AUTHOR
Originally written by boltgolt. Howdy Next is maintained by nathawat on Codeberg.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR pam_howdy (8),
.BR pam (8),
.BR sudo (8)
